1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,640
The harbor ops monolith shipped as one fat
jar for years: berth scheduling, gate
release, and vessel billing lived in the
same process. One deploy window. One
shared database schema. One on-call phone.

2
00:00:14,759 --> 00:00:25,775
Then scheduling needed a Tuesday hotfix
while billing was mid-tariff cutover, and
the release train stalled. Splitting is no
longer a conference talk — it is a
release-calendar problem.

3
00:00:25,896 --> 00:00:36,960
After the cut, three Boot jars stand where
one did: scheduling-service, billing-
service, and gate-service. A truck check-
in that used to be three method calls
becomes three network hops.

4
00:00:37,079 --> 00:00:48,863
A junior engineer hard-codes
http://billing:8082 into RestTemplate. It
works in Compose. Staging uses different
ports.

5
00:00:48,983 --> 00:01:02,039
Tariff YAML is copied into thirty
application.yml files; someone updates the
hazardous-goods surcharge in twenty-nine
of them. When billing slows, gate threads
pile up on HTTP and trucks queue at the
booth.

6
00:01:02,159 --> 00:01:17,015
When a bug spans gateway, gate, and
billing, the logs do not share a request
id. Those are not ideology problems. They
are distributed chores: configuration,
location, routing, resilience, and
observability.

7
00:01:17,135 --> 00:01:30,287
Spring Cloud packages those chores on top
of Spring Boot. Keep the naming straight.
Spring Framework is the container and
modules. Spring Boot is opinionated
single-app delivery.

8
00:01:30,407 --> 00:01:43,655
Spring Cloud assumes Boot apps and adds
multi-service patterns — centralized
config, discovery, API gateways, client-
side load balancing, declarative HTTP
clients, circuit breakers, messaging
binders, tracing hooks.

9
00:01:43,775 --> 00:01:58,079
You do not need every piece on day one.
You need the map so you stop reinventing
each piece with shell scripts. Look at
this java for a moment. Read that
controller as a promise, not finished
architecture.

10
00:01:58,199 --> 00:02:05,351
The method still speaks harbor language —
gate, truck, tariff quote. The
collaborators are remote.

11
00:02:05,471 --> 00:02:13,560
How BillingClient finds a host, how it
fails when billing is down, how tariff
rules reach thirty processes — that is
Cloud territory.

12
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:27,719
Pin versions with the spring-cloud-
dependencies BOM so Boot 3.x and the Cloud
train stay aligned. Half of “Cloud is
hard” is mismatched jars. Spring Cloud is
not a requirement to run Netflix OSS
forever.

13
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:37,343
Eureka, Zuul, and Hystrix shaped early
demos; modern stacks often use Config
Server, Gateway, LoadBalancer, OpenFeign,
Resilience4j,

14
00:02:37,463 --> 00:02:43,199
and Micrometer Tracing — sometimes with
Kubernetes discovery instead of a Java
registry.

15
00:02:43,319 --> 00:03:00,647
The patterns survive the library names.
Config still needs a source of truth.
Instances still need to be found. Truckers
still need one public edge. Calls still
need bulkheads. Spans still need to hop
process boundaries.

16
00:03:00,768 --> 00:03:12,792
A misconception specific to this split is
treating “microservices with Spring” as
“annotate everything @FeignClient and
ship.” Cutting modules without bounded
contexts creates a distributed monolith:

17
00:03:12,912 --> 00:03:18,216
chatty gate↔billing calls, a shared berth
table, and coupled releases.

18
00:03:18,336 --> 00:03:18,696
Another is believing Cloud replaces Boot —
every Cloud service is still a Boot app. A
third is standing up every Cloud component
before the first harbor service ships.

19
00:03:18,816 --> 00:03:34,800
Start with clear boundaries and honest
HTTP or messaging contracts. Add Config
when tariff YAML drift hurts. Add
discovery when hosts stop being static.
Add a gateway when truckers should not
know every internal URL.

20
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:43,536
We named the moment one harbor jar becomes
a fleet, listed the failure modes after
the split, and placed Spring Cloud as the
pattern kit on top of Boot.

21
00:03:43,656 --> 00:03:54,912
The unresolved wiring starts with a boring
operational burn: if thirty services need
the same tariff rules and timeout values,
where does that configuration live so it
does not rot in thirty repositories?

22
00:03:55,092 --> 00:03:58,836
Centralized configuration is the next
pressure.
